    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d346">

                <pb facs="0504" n="246" xml:id="pg246"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 300.115/28385</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Commercial Adviser of the British Embassy</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Crawford</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Foreign Trade
                        Adviser of the Department of State</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Fleming</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-10-24">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">October 24, 1915</hi>.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Mr.</hi>
                    
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Fleming</hi>: With reference to your
                    letter of the 23d instant regarding the voucher or guarantee that would be
                    required, by the British Government on applications from universities, colleges,
                    and public bodies for permits to bring forward scientific or educational works
                    in German, I am to inform you that no more would he required than that every
                    such application should have an endorsement by the official authority appointed
                    that he is satisfied that the application is genuine, that the volumes for which
                    application is made are in fact intended for the use of that institution.</p>
                <p>I should be glad if you would be so kind as to let me know in due course whether
                    the Librarian of Congress would be willing to undertake this service.</p>
                <p>Yours very truly,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">R. F. Crawford</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
